# Start here

This is the evidence one tremula run produced: which planted bugs the project's test suite failed to notice, and what it takes to make one of them fail a test.

Read in this order:

1. `report.json` — every `verdicts` entry whose `verdict` is `survived` is a bug the suite missed.
2. `manifest.json` — the same `mutant_id` under `mutants` gives the file, the byte span, and the exact text the mutation replaces.
3. `patches/<mutant-id>.patch` — that change, ready for `git apply`. A `patch` of `null` in `bundle.json` means no file was written and `patch_error` says why; the diff itself is still in `results.json` under `entries[].diff`.

The run was made outside a version-controlled checkout, so `base.revision` is null and there is no revision to return to. Apply the patch to the project as the run measured it:

    git apply patches/<mutant-id>.patch

Run the project's own default test collection and write a test that fails while the patch is applied. Revert the patch, keep the test, and `tremula run --manifest manifest.json --project <checkout>` will report that mutant as killed. The unmutated suite collected 4 tests; one that collects a different number is not the suite this bundle is about.

What this bundle cannot do is run that suite. It carries the evidence and not the project, so every step above needs a checkout of your own. Paths here are relative to this directory, and `bundle.json` holds the SHA-256 of each file beside its name.
